## 3. The Flow, Step By Step

### Uploading a brief

The user drags one or more files onto the upload page (max 5 files,
5MB each). This gets sent to the server as one request containing all
the files together.

The server then, in order:
1. Checks the person isn't uploading too fast (a safety limit)
2. Checks every file is valid — if even one file in the batch is bad,
   the whole batch is rejected with a clear message, rather than
   silently accepting some and failing on others
3. Uploads each file to Cloudflare's cloud storage (not the database)
4. Creates one tracking record per file, marked "pending"
5. Immediately tells the user "got it, working on it" — without
   making them wait for the AI to actually finish

The actual AI processing then happens separately, after this response
has already gone out.

### Background processing (turning a brief into an outline)

For each file, the server:
1. Downloads the file back from cloud storage
2. Pulls the plain text out of it (different tools are used depending
   on whether it's a `.txt`, `.pdf`, or `.docx` file)
3. Sends that text to Gemini, along with instructions on exactly what
   information to extract and in what format
4. Checks that what Gemini sent back actually matches the expected
   format — if it doesn't, something is wrong, and this is treated as
   a real failure, not just accepted anyway
5. Saves the final result, and marks the tracking record "done" (or
   "failed" if something went wrong)

While this is happening, the tracking record also notes which specific
stage it's currently at ("downloading file," "reading the text,"
"waiting on the AI"), so the interface can show real progress instead
of just a generic spinner.

### Viewing the result

The browser periodically checks in ("is it done yet?") until each
file's outline is ready, then shows it on screen. Each outline also
has "Download as PDF" and "Download as Word" buttons, which generate
those files on the fly when clicked.

### The follow-up action ("Expand this brief")

Clicking this button sends a request referencing the original outline.
The server checks that outline belongs to the user and is actually
finished, creates a new tracking record linked to the original one,
and responds immediately. In the background, DeepSeek is given the
existing outline and asked to expand it into a fuller version — using
the exact same format as before, so it displays the same way. The
result becomes its own separate, downloadable outline.

