## 4. The Data Model

There's one main table involved, called `Job`. Think of a "Job" as one
tracking record — either "process this uploaded brief" or "expand this
existing outline" is one Job each.

**What's stored on each Job:**

- **Who it belongs to** — so a user can never see another user's brief
- **What type it is** — "extraction" (turning a brief into an outline)
  or "follow-up" (expanding an existing outline)
- **Its current status** — pending, processing, done, or failed
- **Which specific stage it's at** — for showing real-time progress
- **How many times it's been attempted** — since a failed attempt
  might get retried once
- **The original uploaded file's name, type, and size** — just
  descriptive info, not the file itself
- **An error message** — filled in only if something went wrong, so
  the user gets an honest reason instead of just "it failed"
- **A reference (key) pointing to the actual file in cloud storage** —
  never the file's actual contents
- **Which AI provider and model actually handled it** — recorded
  after the fact, useful for knowing which model produced which result
- **A link back to the "parent" job** — only set on follow-up jobs, so
  you can trace an expanded brief back to the original it came from
- **The actual result** — the structured outline itself, stored once
  finished

**What makes this safe from mistakes?**

- Every single lookup checks "does this belong to the current user?"
  — so there's no way to view someone else's data even by guessing an
  ID
- A job can only move forward through its stages in one direction
  (pending → processing → done/failed) — it's built so a slow, delayed
  retry can never accidentally overwrite a result that already
  finished successfully
- Before anything is saved as "done," it's checked against the
  expected format first — so a "done" job can never secretly contain
  broken or incomplete data

