## 5. The Concepts

### What an API Endpoint Is

**What it is:** A specific web address on the server that the app's
frontend can send requests to, to do one particular thing — upload a
file, check a job's status, download a result.

**Why it's needed:** The browser itself can't directly reach the
database, the cloud storage, or the AI providers — everything has to
go through the server, and each endpoint is one clearly defined
"thing you can ask the server to do."

**How it was built:** Separate endpoints exist for uploading briefs,
checking a job's status, requesting the expanded version, and
downloading a result as a file. The login/signup endpoints were
already built in the earlier slice and reused unchanged.

**What was avoided:** Doing the actual AI work directly inside the
upload request itself — that would make the user wait, possibly for a
long time, just to get an initial "ok, got it" response.

---

### SDKs vs Raw HTTP

**What it is:** An SDK is a ready-made toolkit a company provides for
talking to their own service correctly. The alternative — "raw HTTP" —
means manually constructing web requests yourself from scratch,
without that toolkit's help.

**Why it's needed:** Each AI provider has its own quirks in how it
expects requests to be formatted and how it reports errors. An
official toolkit already handles all of that correctly; doing it by
hand risks getting small details wrong.

**How it was built:** Google's own official toolkit was used for
Gemini. DeepSeek doesn't provide one of its own, but its service is
compatible with OpenAI's toolkit, so OpenAI's official SDK was used
instead, just pointed at DeepSeek's own address rather than OpenAI's —
this is explicitly allowed for providers that don't have their own SDK,
as long as it's disclosed (which it is, right here).

**What was avoided:** Manually building the requests by hand — that
would just reintroduce all the small mistakes the official toolkits
already prevent.

---

### System Prompts vs User Prompts

**What it is:** A "system prompt" is the fixed instruction that tells
the AI what role to play and exactly how to behave, every single time.
A "user prompt" is the specific piece of data — in this case, the
actual brief text — that changes with each request.

**Why it's needed:** Separating the two means the rules ("extract these
specific things, in this specific format") stay constant, while only
the actual content being processed changes each time.

**How it was built:** Two separate instructions were written — one
telling Gemini exactly what to pull out of a raw brief and in what
shape, and a second telling DeepSeek how to expand an existing outline
into a fuller version, without inventing new facts that weren't
already there.

**What was avoided:** Cramming both the fixed instructions and the
changing data into one single message each time — that mixes up what's
supposed to stay stable with what's supposed to change.

---

### Model Parameters

**What it is:** Adjustable settings that control how an AI model
behaves — how creative versus predictable it is, how long its response
can be, and how long to wait before giving up on a slow response.

**Why it's needed:** Without tuning these, a model might either
produce wildly inconsistent results each time (too creative) or
sound too stiff and repetitive (too predictable) — and without a
maximum wait time, one slow response could freeze things indefinitely.

**How it was built:** For extracting information from a brief, the
"creativity" setting was turned down low, since the same brief should
produce basically the same outline every time — consistency mattered
more than creative flair here. For expanding a brief into fuller,
client-ready writing, the creativity setting was turned up a bit,
since some natural rewording is actually desirable there. Both AI
calls also have a maximum wait time before they're treated as having
failed, so a slow response can't hang forever.

**What was avoided:** Leaving these settings at their defaults or
unbounded — that risks both inconsistent results and calls that never
time out.

---

### Structured Output and Schema Validation

**What it is:** Instead of asking the AI to just write free-form text,
it's asked to return its answer in a specific, predictable shape (like
a form with defined fields) — and then that shape is double-checked in
the app's own code before it's trusted.

**Why it's needed:** Free-form text can't be neatly displayed on
screen or turned into a downloadable document. And even when an AI is
asked for a specific format, it can still occasionally get it slightly
wrong — so relying on the AI alone to enforce its own format isn't
enough.

**How it was built:** Both AI calls are asked to return their answer
as structured data matching an expected shape. After getting a
response, the app checks it itself against that same expected shape.
For the initial extraction, if it doesn't match, the app tries asking
again once, with a firmer reminder. If it still doesn't match on that
retry, the job is marked as genuinely failed, with a clear
explanation — rather than saving something broken and pretending it
succeeded. The follow-up (expansion) call does not retry on a format
mismatch; it fails the job honestly on the first mismatch, since it
receives a cleaner, already-structured outline to work from.

**What was avoided:** Simply trusting whatever the AI sends back
without double-checking it — that risks silently saving corrupted or
incomplete data as if it were a success.

---

### Jobs and Workers

**What it is:** A "job" is a record of "something needs to happen."
A "worker" is the part of the code that actually does that work,
separately from the original request that created the job.

**Why it's needed:** AI responses can take anywhere from a few seconds
to over a minute. Making the user's browser sit and wait the entire
time for that to finish would be a poor experience, and would tie up
server resources unnecessarily.

**How it was built:** As soon as a file is uploaded, a job record is
created and the user gets an instant reply — the actual AI work then
happens afterward, in the background, updating that same job record as
it progresses. The browser simply checks back periodically to see if
it's done yet.

**What was avoided:** Processing the AI call directly inside the
original upload request — that would mean the user staring at a
loading spinner tied directly to how fast (or slow) an external AI
service happens to respond that particular moment.

---

### Queues, and Why Concurrency Is Capped

**What it is:** A limit on how many AI requests are allowed to be
"in flight" (actively waiting for a response) at the exact same time,
no matter how many files someone uploads at once.

**Why it's needed:** If someone uploads, say, 20 files at once with no
limit, that could fire off 20 simultaneous requests to the AI
provider all at once — which risks running up costs quickly and can
get temporarily blocked by the provider for sending too much traffic
too fast.

**How it was built:** No more than 2 AI requests are allowed to be
actively in progress at once, system-wide — everything else waits its
turn in line until a slot opens up. This limit applies across all
users combined, not per individual person, since it's protecting one
shared connection to the AI provider.

**What was avoided:** Building this queuing/limiting logic completely
from scratch — instead, a small, well-tested existing tool was used
for it, since reinventing this kind of thing tends to introduce subtle
bugs.

---

### Rate Limiting as a Cost Control

**What it is:** Restricting how often certain actions can be repeated
in a short time window — both by an individual user, and overall,
system-wide.

**Why it's needed:** Every single AI call costs real money. Without
any limits, someone repeatedly hammering the upload or "expand" button
translates directly into real, uncontrolled spending.

**How it was built:** There are two separate layers of limits. One
limits how often any single person can upload files or request an
expansion. A second, separate limit caps the *total* number of
requests sent to each AI provider overall, across every user
combined — since the real risk being protected against is the shared
account's usage limit being exhausted, not just one person acting
alone. If the AI provider itself says "slow down, try again in X
seconds," that exact wait time is respected before trying again.

**What was avoided:** Only limiting individual users and assuming
that's enough — many users acting normally, all at once, could still
add up to the same problem, so an overall system-wide limit exists on
top of the individual ones.

---

### Why Files Live in Cloud Storage, Not the Database

**What it is:** Actual uploaded files are stored in a dedicated cloud
storage service (Cloudflare, in this case), and only a short reference
pointing to that file is kept in the database — never the file itself.

**Why it's needed:** Databases are built and optimized for organized,
structured records — not for holding large files. Stuffing files
directly into a database tends to slow it down and complicates things
like backups.

**How it was built:** Every uploaded file goes straight to Cloudflare
storage. The database only ever stores a short "key" — basically an
address — pointing to where that file actually lives.

**What was avoided:** Saving the raw uploaded file directly inside the
database — this would bloat it unnecessarily and isn't what a
database like this is meant for.

---

### The Cost Model

**What it is:** A rough, honest estimate of what each individual AI
request actually costs in real money, so the limits already put in
place can be judged against real numbers rather than guesses.

**Why it's needed:** AI usage costs can be invisible until a surprising
bill shows up — knowing roughly what one run costs is what makes a
sensible limit actually sensible.

**How it was built:** Based on typical usage, extracting one outline
from a brief costs a small fraction of a cent, and expanding one
outline into a fuller version costs about the same. Combined with the
existing limits on how many requests can run at once and how often
they can repeat, the realistic worst-case spending stays well under a
dollar, even under fairly heavy use.

**What was avoided:** Adding a hard spending cap that would completely
block usage the moment some fixed dollar limit is hit — the existing
combination of limits already keeps costs low enough that a harsh
cutoff risked blocking a legitimate user over a false alarm.

