## 6. What Went Wrong

**Problem 1 — Installing the project failed with a permissions error**

*What happened:* Running the install command failed, complaining it
didn't have permission to create certain files.

*What was checked:* At first it looked like a simple settings mixup —
the tool's cache location had accidentally been pointed at a folder
belonging to a completely different project. Fixing that revealed a
second, deeper issue underneath.

*What was actually wrong:* Some of the cached files were owned by the
computer's admin account instead of the normal user account — likely
because the install tool had been run with elevated admin permissions
by accident at some point in the past, which is generally something to
avoid doing.

*How it was fixed:* The broken cache setting was reset, and ownership
of the affected folder was corrected back to the normal user account.

---

**Problem 2 — PDF files failed to process, but text and Word files worked fine**

*What happened:* Uploading a `.pdf` file consistently failed during
processing, while `.txt` and `.docx` files worked without any issue.

*What was checked:* The failure was traced specifically to the part of
the code responsible for reading text out of PDF files.

*What was actually wrong:* The tool used for reading PDFs relies on a
separate helper file to do some of its work, and the specific way the
project's build process was set up caused it to look for that helper
file in the wrong location, so it couldn't find it.

*How it was fixed:* The code was adjusted to find and load that helper
file directly, rather than relying on the default (and, in this setup,
broken) way of locating it.

---

**Problem 3 — The AI occasionally returned data in the wrong shape**

*What happened:* Every once in a while, even though the AI request
itself succeeded normally, the actual content it returned didn't match
the expected structure — sometimes missing pieces entirely.

*What was checked:* This wasn't a connection or server error — the
request itself always succeeded. The problem was specifically that
the content inside a successful response didn't match what was
expected.

*What was actually wrong:* Even when explicitly asked to return data
in a specific format, the AI model doesn't always follow that format
perfectly every single time.

*How it was fixed:* Every response is checked against the expected
format before being trusted. If it doesn't match, the request is tried
one more time with a firmer reminder about the required format. If it
still doesn't match after that second attempt, the whole thing is
honestly marked as failed, rather than silently saving something
broken.

---

**Problem 4 — Downloading certain briefs as a PDF crashed**

*What happened:* Downloading a brief as a PDF failed specifically when
the brief's content contained emoji, non-English characters, or
special symbols.

*What was checked:* The error was tracked down to the exact step where
the PDF file gets finalized and saved.

*What was actually wrong:* The specific text styles being used for
PDFs only support a limited set of characters (essentially standard
English characters and basic symbols) — anything outside that set
caused the whole PDF generation to fail completely.

*How it was fixed:* Any character outside that supported set now gets
automatically swapped out for a simple placeholder character before
the PDF is generated, so PDF downloads always succeed — even for
briefs containing emoji or non-English text. (Word document downloads
were never affected by this, since they support a much wider range of
characters natively.)

---

**Problem 5 — The expanded brief's title showed up twice**

*What happened:* After using the "expand this brief" feature, the same
project title appeared twice, stacked right on top of each other, on
the results page.

*What was checked:* It turned out the expanded result was being
displayed using the exact same display component as the original
outline — including the part of that component responsible for
showing the big title header.

*What was actually wrong:* Since the expanded brief keeps the same
title as the original, and both were being shown using the same
"show everything including the title" display logic, the title ended
up rendering twice — once from the original, and again from the
expanded version directly underneath it.

*How it was fixed:* The display component was given a simple on/off
option to hide just the title header. The expanded version now uses
that option to hide its own duplicate title, showing only its content
underneath its own separate "Expanded brief" label instead.

