## 7. What This Slice Does Not Handle

**What would break under heavy real-world use:**

- If the server restarts while files are mid-processing, those jobs
  would get stuck showing "processing" forever, with nothing left to
  actually finish them.
- If a large number of files were uploaded all at once, while the
  number of actual AI requests is safely limited, other work (like
  downloading files and reading their text) isn't limited the same
  way, and could use up a lot of memory all at once.
- The current safety limits (rate limits and usage caps) only work
  correctly if the app is running as a single instance — running
  multiple copies of the app at once would let each copy track its own
  separate limits, effectively allowing several times more usage than
  intended.

**What would be added before real users relied on this:**

- A more robust, dedicated system for managing background jobs, with
  proper retry timing and a way to review jobs that permanently failed
  instead of just leaving them marked "failed."
- Making the current safety limits shared across multiple running
  copies of the app, instead of each one tracking its own separately.
- A way for a user to manually retry a brief that failed, and a way to
  clean up stored files for jobs that are deleted or no longer needed.
- An overall monthly spending safety net, once real paying users are
  involved.

**Left out on purpose, versus left out due to time:**

- Deliberately left out (not part of what this slice was meant to
  cover): editing results, sharing them with others, and viewing a
  history of every past brief in one place.
- Left out due to time constraints: filtering or paging through a list
  of past jobs, and a more advanced job-review system — the current
  simple approach honestly covers what was required, just not in the
  most production-hardened way possible.

---

## 8. If I Built This Again

The one thing worth doing differently from the start: use a proper,
dedicated background job system from day one, instead of the simpler
"just handle it right away in the background" approach used here.
The current approach works and is easy to understand, but it pushed
extra complexity into other places instead — the interface has to keep
checking back repeatedly to see if something's done, and if the server
ever restarts partway through, there's no way for an unfinished job to
pick back up where it left off. A proper job system would have handled
all of that automatically from the start, at the cost of one extra
piece of infrastructure to set up — which is something any serious,
long-term version of this feature would eventually need anyway.

